="feather feather-chevron-down" style="fill: none;stroke: currentColor;width: var(--rsw, 100%);height: 100%" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg></span></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:Rsb2:"><div data-rid="7a63ca96-0810-4c64-8fff-b62fefb0656b" class="r-1qwy9kk"><div data-rid="e5663a9a-9696-4db2-bc7c-957b3d08547b" class="r-v48jzs alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>The information provided on this website, through our products, and during consultations is intended for general wellness and educational purposes only. This product is not intended to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent any disease or medical condition. The claims made here have not been evaluated by any regulatory authority. <br><br>Our health professionals and consultations are designed to provide general wellness guidance and should not replace advice from your healthcare provider. Always consult with a physician, pharmacist, or other qualified healthcare professional for questions regarding your health, medications, or specific medical conditions.<br><br><br>Important Information on Use: <br><br>1. Results may vary based on individual factors such as starting health, lifestyle, adherence to recommendations, and other unique circumstances.<br><br>2. Proper diet, exercise, and hydration are essential for achieving and maintaining health benefits.<br><br>3. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding, It is not recommended to take monthlies.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="d1c1ce1c-627a-4af4-8692-13b0e604c371" class="r-nrguxy" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed" data-orientation="vertical"><div data-rid="72d9cf73-da10-4583-8655-f06479327ab5" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="replo-:R1cb2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1v9ld38"><div data-rid="9e1d0e9c-0c8b-481a-be01-5f62f01c7d1b" class="r-1ptpyp alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Study References:</p></span></div><div data-rid="10311970-8075-4b3c-8881-f9840009b42c" class="r-11ukx49"><span style="display:contents"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="feather feather-chevron-down" style="fill: none;stroke: currentColor;width: var(--rsw, 100%);height: 100%" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg></span></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R1cb2:"><div data-rid="46640398-914f-4bdf-b6df-c9c046fc8b64" class="r-12cdvmm"><div data-rid="ecccede4-91ba-4e67-834e-ff3a20cabdb2" class="r-1ksevwh alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>The impact of menstrual symptoms on everyday life: a survey among 42,879 women Harlow, S. D., & Park, M. (1996). American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology, 174(2), 683-689. https://www.ajog.org/article/S0002-9378(19)30427-2/abstract<br><br>NSAID Resistance in Dysmenorrhea: Epidemiology, Causes, and Treatment Options "A review of 51 clinical trials found that 18% of women report minimal or no relief of menstrual pain with NSAIDs." https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5839921/<br><br>NSAID use is associated with increased risk of acute kidney injury, particularly in older adults and those with chronic conditions. https://bjgpopen.org/content/6/1/BJGPO.2021.0208.long<br><br>Women with dysmenorrhea using NSAIDs had a 2.29-fold higher risk of stroke with prolonged use (≥24 days/month). https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0259047<br><br>32% of women using NSAIDs for period pain reported gastrointestinal side effects, including nausea, ulcers, and diarrhea." https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/14/6/e079660<br><br>Stanford University researchers found that ibuprofen use during ultramarathons doubled the risk of acute kidney injury compared to a placebo. For every five runners taking ibuprofen, one additional case of kidney injury occurred. https://med.stanford.edu/news/all-news/2017/07/pain-reliever-linked-to-kidney-injury-in-endurance-runners.html<br><br>Chronic Stress and Ovulatory Dysfunction: Implications in Times of COVID-19 Vigil, P., Meléndez, J., Soto, H., Petkovic, G., Bernal, Y. A., & Molina, S. (2022). Frontiers in Global Women's Health, 3, Article 866104. https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/global-womens-health/articles/10.3389/fgwh.2022.866104/full<br><br>Micronutrient deficiencies among women of reproductive age: a global and regional assessment Kothari, M. T., et al. (2024). The Lancet Global Health, 12(1), e76-e84. https://www.thelancet.com/journals/langlo/article/PIIS2214-109X(24)00276-6/fulltext<br><br>Houston, B. L., Hurrie, D., Graham, J., Perija, B., Rimmer, E., Rabbani, R., Bernstein, C. N., Turgeon, A. F., Fergusson, D. A., Houston, D. S., Abou-Setta, A. M., & Zarychanski, R. (2018). Efficacy of iron supplementation on fatigue and physical capacity in non-anaemic iron-deficient adults: a systematic review of randomised controlled trials. BMJ Open, 8(4), e019240. https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/8/4/e019240<br><br>Ebrahimi, E., Khayati Motlagh, S., Nemati, S., Tavakoli, Z., & Hojat Yar, M. (2012). Effects of magnesium and vitamin B6 on the severity of premenstrual syndrome symptoms. Journal of Caring Sciences, 1(4), 183–189. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4161081/<br><br>Fathizadeh, N., Ebrahimi, E., Valiani, M., Tavakoli, N., Hojat Yar, M., & Hojat Yar, M. (2010). Evaluating the effect of magnesium and magnesium plus vitamin B6 supplement on the severity of premenstrual syndrome. Iranian Journal of Nursing and Midwifery Research, 15(Suppl1), 401–405. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3208934/#ref5 <br><br>Wyatt, K. M., Dimmock, P. W., Jones, P. W., & O'Brien, P. M. (1999). Efficacy of vitamin B-6 in the treatment of premenstrual syndrome: systematic review. BMJ, 318(7195), 1375–1381. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC27878/#sec14 <br><br>James W. Daily, Ph.D., Xin Zhang, BS, Da Sol Kim, MS, Sunmin Park, Ph.D., Efficacy of Ginger for Alleviating the Symptoms of Primary Dysmenorrhea: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Randomized Clinical Trials, Pain Medicine, Volume 16, Issue 12, December 2015, Pages 2243–2255, https://doi.org/10.1111/pme.12853<br><br>Rose, D. P. (1978). The interactions between vitamin B6 and hormones. Vitamins and Hormones, 36, 53–99. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/217175/<br><br><a target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow noreferrer" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0708/7462/8371/files/studies_final.pdf?v=1731106438"><u>See all here.</u></a></p></span></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><style>@import url(https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Manrope:wght@100;200;300;400;500;600;700;800;900;950&family=Quicksand:wght@100;200;300;400;500;600;700;800;900;950&family=Quattrocento+Sans:wght@100;200;300;400;500;600;700;800;900;950&family=Lora:wght@100;200;300;400;500;600;700;800;900;950)</style></div>
